<h2>A real market where testing discipline matters more than volume</h2>
<p>At roughly USD 132 million in 2024, the US is a genuinely significant chilli market for India — well behind China's scale, but a real, established line. What sets this market apart isn't the volume, it's the regulatory attention: spices generally, and chilli among them, have drawn FDA focus for Salmonella and pesticide-residue testing, with import alerts capable of placing a specific product or exporter under automatic detention. That risk profile, more than shipping logistics, is what defines how we run US chilli orders.</p>

<h2>Pre-shipment testing is standard, not a discretionary extra</h2>
<p>Given that risk, we treat pre-shipment testing to US-relevant residue and Salmonella limits as the default for US-bound chilli rather than something a buyer has to specifically request. A detention after a 25-35 day transit is a far more expensive and disruptive problem than catching an issue before the container is sealed — the math on testing cost versus detention risk is not close.</p>

<h2>FSVP adds paperwork, not shipping delay</h2>
<p>Your US importer's Foreign Supplier Verification Program obligation means more documentation exchange with us than most other markets require — process information, specifications, supporting records. It doesn't slow the shipping timeline itself, but it does mean starting that documentation conversation early rather than treating it as a formality that can be handled while the container is already at port.</p>

<h2>Variety and form follow end use</h2>
<p>US buyers split fairly clearly by end use — Teja for heat in food manufacturing, Byadgi where colour extraction or oleoresin production is the goal — and whole versus crushed/ground depends entirely on the buyer's own processing setup. Tell us specifically what your chilli is going into and we'll recommend the right variety and form rather than quoting generic "export-grade chilli."</p>

